       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="pst-vs-pdt" class="reveal" aria-label="PST vs PDT Explained">
    <h2>2. PST vs PDT Explained</h2>
    <p>
      The distinction between Pacific Standard Time (PST) and Pacific Daylight Time (PDT) is crucial for accurate scheduling. Applying the wrong term can cause significant confusion, as standard and daylight offsets represent a difference of exactly one hour.
    </p>
    
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Time Type</th>
            <th>Abbreviation</th>
            <th>UTC Offset</th>
            <th>Typical Months of Observance</th>
            <th>Clock Action</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Pacific Standard Time</td>
            <td>PST</td>
            <td>UTC-8</td>
            <td>November to March</td>
            <td>Standard winter time (Clocks fall back 1 hour)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Pacific Daylight Time</td>
            <td>PDT</td>
            <td>UTC-7</td>
            <td>March to November</td>
            <td>Daylight saving time (Clocks spring forward 1 hour)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Daylight Saving Time Transition Dates</h3>
    <p>
      Under federal law in the United States (the Uniform Time Act of 1966), clock changes occur at 2:00 a.m. local time:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Spring Transition (DST Starts):</strong> On the second Sunday of March, clocks are advanced from 2:00 a.m. to 3:00 a.m. local standard time, transitioning the region into Pacific Daylight Time (PDT, UTC-7).</li>
      <li><strong>Autumn Transition (DST Ends):</strong> On the first Sunday of November, clocks are set back from 2:00 a.m. to 1:00 a.m. local daylight time, returning the region to Pacific Standard Time (PST, UTC-8).</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Warning Box -->
    <div class="warning-box">
      <h3>Watch Out for The Offset Change</h3>
      <p>
        Writing "PST" in July is technically incorrect and can cause confusion for automated systems or international schedulers. During the summer, Los Angeles is strictly on PDT.
      </p>
    </div>

