  <section id="programming-tech" class="reveal" aria-label="Programming and Cloud Tech">
    <h2>7. Programming, Databases &amp; Cloud Infrastructure</h2>
    <p>
      For developers and cloud engineers, managing local timezones in software logic requires discipline. Incorrect scheduling can lead to broken database records, logging conflicts, and failing cron jobs during Daylight Saving Time transition windows.
    </p>

    <h3>Cloud Server Location Offsets</h3>
    <p>
      Many major cloud platforms host resources on the West Coast of the United States (such as AWS <code>us-west-1</code> in Northern California or <code>us-west-2</code> in Oregon). Servers running within these regions are often set to UTC by default. Setting servers to localized Pacific Time can introduce issues when calculating database logs, making UTC the standard choice for runtime environments.
    </p>

    <h3>ISO 8601 Format Representation</h3>
    <p>
      When passing timestamps in APIs, always use ISO 8601 formatting. The correct representations for Pacific Standard and Pacific Daylight times are:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Standard Time Offset (PST):</strong> <code>2026-12-15T09:30:00-08:00</code></li>
      <li><strong>Daylight Saving Time Offset (PDT):</strong> <code>2026-06-15T09:30:00-07:00</code></li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Did You Know Box -->
    <div class="did-you-know">
      <h4>Database Tip</h4>
      <p>
        Always store timestamp data in UTC within your database tables (e.g., using <code>TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE</code> in PostgreSQL). Perform the conversion to <code>America/Los_Angeles</code> only in the application presentation layer to ensure that DST changes are resolved dynamically based on historical and current rules.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

